jobs.instructions:
Candidates should submit the following materials: 

(1) a full teaching dossier, including: (a) complete quantitative student evaluations for multiple courses;  (b) sample syllabi, including at least one for an Introduction to Philosophy lecture course; (c) a sample single-class lesson plan from the Intro course; 

(2) a brief (<250 words) statement on your pedagogical approach to AI in teaching, especially in large learning environments, including enforcement practices; 

(3) a teaching statement (<1000 words) that includes discussion of the following: (a) your ability and preparation to teach ​​large classes that are intellectually challenging to a selective student body; (b) your approach to fostering engagement and participatory learning among students taking the course to satisfy a university core requirement (https://corecurriculum.nd.edu/our-core-curriculum/ways-of-knowing/philosophy/); (c) your approach to managing a team of teaching assistants to ensure consistency of grading and feedback; (d) your proposed learning goals for an introductory philosophy course at a university with Notre Dame's distinctive mission.

(4) two teaching-focused letters of recommendation

(5) a CV

A full job description and details of how to apply are available at https://jobs.leeds.ac.uk/Vacancy.aspx?ref=AHCPR1088. An online application form will ask you to evidence your qualifications regarding the essential and desirable criteria for the post. A separate cover letter is not necessary. Your application should also include the following (in this order) as a single PDF: 

1. A standard academic CV; 
2. A description of future research plans (1-2 pages), e.g. elaboration of future research projects, publication plans, and/or grant application or non-academic impact plans; 
3. A recent research writing sample (maximum 10,000 words).
